EXTRA3 = {
"ai-seo-for-small-business": [("What a good result looks like", "<p>Success in AI search is not a single ranking. It looks like this: when someone in your area asks an assistant the questions your customers ask, your business is named, the details about you are correct, and the reason given matches what you are genuinely good at. Alongside that, you see more visits from AI assistants in Google Analytics and more calls that mention finding you through an AI answer. Track the same ten questions every month and you will see the trend, even though individual answers vary from one day to the next.</p>")],
"google-business-profile-optimization-orange-county": [("Profiles for businesses with staff in the field", "<p>Plumbers, cleaners, mobile mechanics and other trades with crews on the road should still keep one profile for the central location with a service area, and add photos of vehicles, uniforms and completed work in different cities. Those photos show customers who will arrive and give both Google and AI assistants evidence that you really work across the area you claim.</p>")],
"how-to-get-more-google-reviews": [("Building a steady review habit", "<p>The businesses with the strongest review profiles rarely run review campaigns. They make asking part of every job: the request goes out automatically after each appointment, staff mention it at the end of each visit, and the manager checks new reviews weekly and replies. A steady flow of recent reviews looks more trustworthy to customers than a burst followed by months of silence, and it keeps your profile current for both Google and AI assistants. Set a simple monthly target, such as asking every customer and replying to every review, rather than a target number of reviews, which can tempt staff to cut corners.</p>")],
"how-to-respond-to-google-reviews": [("Building a reply routine", "<p>Pick a regular time each week to read and reply to new reviews, and make one person responsible. Keep a short document of approved phrases, the tone you want and topics to avoid, especially for regulated businesses. Escalate serious complaints to a manager the same day. Over time, review replies become a record of how you treat customers, which prospective customers read before calling.</p>")],
"is-seo-worth-it-for-small-business": [("How to decide in one afternoon", "<p>Search for your main service and city on Google and ask the same question to ChatGPT. If competitors appear and you do not, there is demand you are missing. Check your Business Profile: if it is incomplete or rarely updated, there is easy work to do. Look at your call records: if you cannot tell which calls came from search, set up tracking first. If customers search for what you sell and your basics are weak, SEO is very likely worth it.</p>")],
"local-landing-pages": [("Questions to ask before you publish", "<p>Before publishing any city page, ask: would a customer in this city learn something here they could not learn on the main service page? Is there real work, a team or a review from this city to show? Would I be comfortable if a Google reviewer compared this page with my other city pages? If the honest answer to any of these is no, improve the main service page instead, and let your Business Profile service area cover the city.</p>")],
"local-seo-checklist": [("How long each stage usually takes", "<p>Profile fixes can be done in an afternoon, though verification can take longer. Listing cleanup usually takes a few weeks, because some platforms are slow to process changes. Website improvements depend on how many services you offer; one good service page might take a day to write and check. Reviews and local links build over months. Tracking can be set up in a day and should be in place before you judge whether anything is working.</p>")],
"local-seo-keywords": [("Questions that make better pages", "<p>Some of the most valuable local keywords are questions asked just before someone buys: how long does it take, how much does it usually cost, do you need a permit, what happens on the first visit. Answering these clearly on your service pages helps you match long searches in Google, gives AI assistants something specific to quote and reassures customers enough to call.</p>")],
"nap-consistency-local-citations": [("Keeping a listing log", "<p>Keep a simple spreadsheet with every platform where your business is listed, the login owner, the exact details shown and the date you last checked. When anything changes, work through the list in order. This turns a stressful cleanup into a routine task that takes an hour, and makes it easy to hand over if staff or agencies change.</p>")],
"near-me-searches": [("Where near me searches happen", "<p>Near me searches happen in Google Search, Google Maps, Apple Maps, voice assistants and AI chat apps. Each draws on business listings and websites, so your profile on Google, your Apple Maps and Bing listings and your own website all need the same accurate details. A business that is correct on Google but wrong on Apple Maps can lose iPhone users searching for directions.</p>"),
  ("Turning near me visibility into customers", "<p>Appearing is only half the job. Near me searchers decide quickly, so make the next step easy: a tappable phone number, clear opening hours, accurate directions, a short description of what makes you different and recent reviews. Check your profile from a phone in the area and ask whether a stranger could choose you in thirty seconds.</p>")],
"service-area-business-seo": [("Growing into a new area the honest way", "<p>When you want customers in a new city, start by taking jobs there, ask those customers for reviews that mention the city, and add the projects to your website with photos. Extend your Business Profile service area once you genuinely serve it. This builds the evidence Google and AI assistants look for, and it avoids the risks of fake locations, which can lead to suspended profiles.</p>")],
}
